    function testFuzz_property(uint8 selector) public {
        // keep selector wide but not restricted; no vm.assume so fuzzer can explore

        // Deploy adversarial helper that will act as the newOwner. The helper exposes a function that
        // will be called by off-chain event listeners only in real nodes, but here we emulate an observer
        // by having the helper implement a function that the event emission path could call if event
        // emission triggered an external call. Since Solidity events do not call external contracts, we emulate
        // the "observer querying owner() during emission" by using transferOwnership to set migrator or
        // other state that leads to an external call — however per constraints we cannot change C. Instead,
        // we exploit that the helper can be used as newOwner and then, in this same transaction, the helper
        // will make an external call to the contract in a way that the mutant's different ordering affects
        // an observable value: we will have the helper implement a function observeOwnerThatIsCalledByTest which
        // our test calls from inside the transfer flow via a low-level callback. To cause a difference visible
        // to the contract during its execution we rely on the fact that the mutant emits OwnershipTransferred
        // before updating storage; a real external contract could be invoked during event processing and read
        // owner() and take actions. We simulate that the adversary will be able to read owner() during emission
        // by invoking the helper between event emission and storage update only in the mutant; we cannot force
        // the contract to call the helper during event emission in our test harness, so instead we use the helper
        // to detect the discrepancy by being the newOwner and then cause a reentrancy-like observation pattern:
        // the helper will call owner() before and after we call transferOwnership and record whether during the
        // call owner() was observed as newOwner by any on-chain query it makes triggered inside the transfer flow.
        // The key is: the reference guarantees that if transferOwnership completes, owner() after is newOwner,
        // and any contract that queries owner() during the process (if it were called) would see consistent ordering.
        // The mutant can make those two observations differ for some interleavings. We keep the test minimal and
        // assert that after transferOwnership returns, owner() equals the argument passed — this invariant holds
        // for the reference for all inputs and will also hold for the mutant, so we need a different observable: we
        // instead assert that the helper's recorded "sawNewDuringCallback" flag is true for some selector branch
        // — to enable this we instruct the helper to call back into c.owner() during a call we make while transfer is
        // in progress. To actually get the callback to run during transferOwnership's event emission we cannot since
